parts.text:
Now I understand the structure well. Design decision for the scoped helper:

A Proxy-based helper `scoped(prisma, tenantId)` that intercepts model method calls and merges `tenantId` into `where` (for finds/updates/deletes) and into `data` (for creates). This makes conversion mechanical: replace `prisma` with `scoped(prisma, req)` in route handlers.

BUT there's a subtlety: helper functions outside route handlers (like `notifyStaffOnGuardianAction`) don't have `req`. They'd receive tenantId as param. I'll pass tenantId to those helpers.

The middleware: `authenticate` becomes async and resolves membership + tenant status. It sets `req.user.tenantId` and `req.user.tenantRole`.

Types: add `tenantId?: string` and `tenantRole?: string` to Express.User.

BLOQUEADO → 403 with message "Assinatura vencida" style.

Design the proxy precisely:

```ts
import type { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';

const TENANT_MODELS = new Set([...]); // model names with tenantId

export function scoped(prisma: PrismaClient, tenantId?: string) {
  const tid = tenantId || '';
  return new Proxy(prisma as any, {
    get(target, prop) {
      const model = (target as any)[prop];
      if (!model || typeof model !== 'object') return model;
      if (!TENANT_MODELS.has(String(prop))) return model;
      return new Proxy(model, {
        get(t, m) {
          const method = (t as any)[m];
          if (typeof method !== 'function') return method;
          const name = String(m);
          return (...args: any[]) => {
            const [arg0] = args;
            if (name === 'create') {
              return method.call(t, { ...arg0, data: { ...(arg0?.data || {}), tenantId: arg0?.data?.tenantId ?? tid } });
            }
            if (name === 'createMany') {
              return method.call(t, { ...arg0, data: arg0?.data?.map ? arg0.data.map((r: any) => ({...r, tenantId: r.tenantId ?? tid})) : arg0?.data });
            }
            if (['findMany','findFirst','count','aggregate','groupBy','updateMany','deleteMany'].includes(name)) {
              return method.call(t, { ...arg0, where: { ...(arg0?.where || {}), tenantId: arg0?.where?.tenantId ?? tid } });
            }
            if (['update','delete','updateMany2','findUnique'...]) — wait for update/delete:
              return method.call(t, { ...arg0, where: { ...(arg0?.where || {}), tenantId: tid } });
            if (name === 'upsert') {
              return method.call(t, {
                ...arg0,
                where: { ...(arg0?.where || {}), tenantId: tid },
                create: { ...(arg0?.create || {}), tenantId: arg0?.create?.tenantId ?? tid },
                update: arg0?.update,
              });
            }
            return method.call(t, ...args);
          };
        }
      });
    }
  });
}
```

Wait — for `update`/`delete` with `where: { id: X }`, adding `tenantId` makes it `where: { id: X, tenantId: Y }` — Prisma ANDs the fields in where for models with scalar fields. Actually for a non-unique compound where without @@unique, Prisma's `update` requires a unique where... `where: { id: X, tenantId: Y }` — Prisma accepts non-unique where for update only if... no: update requires `where: PacienteWhereUniqueInput`. Passing `{id, tenantId}` where tenantId is NOT part of a unique constraint → type error and runtime error "filter on non-unique fields is not allowed for update". YES — important gotcha! Prisma update/delete require unique where. So `where: { id, tenantId }` fails at runtime.

Solution: use `updateMany`/`deleteMany` for scoped writes, or keep update with `where: { id }` but first verify ownership via findFirst({id, tenantId}). The latter is 2 queries but safe; the former is 1 query but returns no row count semantics... updateMany returns {count}, not the row — breaks code expecting the updated row.

Practical approach in the proxy: 
- `update`: fetch ownership? Can't await inside sync mutator signature... The proxy returns an async function, so it CAN await: 
```ts
if (name === 'update') {
  return (async (...args) => {
    await checkOwnership(target, tid, where) // findFirst id+tenantId, throw if missing
    return method.call(t, ...args);
  })();
}
```
But `checkOwnership` on every update adds a query — acceptable for this app scale, and *correct*. Actually simpler variant used by many SaaS: convert update to updateMany + re-fetch... updateMany can't set scalar in where+return. Simplest robust: ownership-check then original update. Fine.

But wait — Prisma's `update` where must be unique but `{id}` is unique; adding tenantId breaks it. Also for update where we could pass `where: { id: X }` AND ALSO `data: { tenantId }`? No — that would move the row to the wrong tenant... only if tenant ID differs. Idempotent anyway. But checking ownership first is the correct isolation guarantee.

Hmm, simpler alternative that keeps 1 query: `update` proxy → `method.updateMany({ where: {id, tenantId}, data })` then if count===0 throw/404... but loses returned row. Callers use returned row (`res.json(updated)`). I could follow-up `findUnique({where:{id}})` — 2 queries too. Ownership-check + update is also 2. OK do ownership-check + original update. Actually — even simpler: perform `findUnique({ where: { id } })`... no wait, that leaks existence; but we check tenant match. Fine.

For `delete`: same ownership check then delete.

For `deleteMany`/`updateMany`: merge tenantId into where (no change to semantics) ✓.

For `findUnique`: merge tenantId into where → `findUnique` also requires unique where... `findUnique({where:{id, tenantId}})`?? Same issue. findUnique requires WhereUniqueInput. BUT `findFirst` accepts non-unique. So convert findUnique → findFirst? Return semantics identical (single record). The proxy can transparently map findUnique to findFirst with merged tenantId. 

Also `upsert`: where must be unique → convert to: findFirst(where + tenantId); if found update, else create. Implement manually in proxy:
```ts
upsert: async ({where, create, update}) => {
  const existing = await model.findFirst({ where: { ...where, tenantId: tid } });
  if (existing) return model.update({ where: { id: existing.id }, data: update });
  return model.create({ data: { ...create, tenantId: ... } });
}
```
Caveat: where for upsert usually `{ uniqueField: val }`; findFirst with that + tenantId works.

In transaction scripts (`$transaction`), the proxy intercepts `prisma.$transaction`? TENANT_MODELS only for models; `$transaction` is a function — passes through untouched (fine, transaction usages only appear in seed/backfill or specific places — check later).

The `groupBy` needs `by` + where merge — fine. `aggregate` fine.

Edge: `prisma.$queryRaw` / raw — passthrough.

Now the "create" merge: `data.tenantId = tid` unless provided.

Wrapped model prop access also appears INSIDE other libs (e.g., `lib/email`, `config/passport` use prisma directly — passport creates users/verification/social accounts — those models aren't tenant models mostly; SocialAccount/User/VerificationCode unscoped ✓).

Now, which files import prisma from '../lib/prisma' and use tenant models: all routes. Non-tenant models used in routes: User (users.ts, permissions.ts, auth.ts), SocialAccount, VerificationCode (auth.ts). Those shouldn't be proxied (User has no tenantId). TENANT_MODELS set handles this.

One more: `notifications.ts` — Notification has tenantId ✓ but notifications are per-user; scoping by tenantId is still correct (staff notifications belong to their membership tenant).

