The errorHandler is genuinely never invoked. The default Express finalhandler renders the HTML.

Now — why would the app-level errorHandler not be found? Let me look at index.ts... `app.use('/api', routes)` then `app.use(errorHandler)`. 

OH WAIT!!! I think I've got it. Look at my async-express `use` patch:

```ts
(Router as any)[m] = function (this: any, path: any, ...handlers: any[]) {
  const wrapped = handlers.map((h: any) => (typeof h === 'function' ? wrap(h) : h));
  return orig.call(this, path, ...wrapped);
};
```

This is fine for routers. But in Express 4, **`app.use` is NOT `Router.use`** — correct, app.use is separate.

BUT — `routes/index.ts`'s router mounts `router.use('/api', routes)`... no wait, `app.use('/api', routes)`.

Hmm, but what about `router.use((req,res,next)=>...)`? Not relevant.

